</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-based query rewriters in production face a tension: the training reward must reflect how the rewrite is consumed by the production ranker, yet the training procedure must be cheap enough to support continuous redeployment as data drifts. We present <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoRe</span> (Context Relevance), such a system, redeployed weekly for over five months in a major short-video search engine. Our reward uses the deployed multimodal relevance model as its source and a multiplicative ratio form mirroring the production fusion algebra, closing the simulation–production gap that offline reward proxies leave open. A semi-online Mixed Preference Optimization loop makes this reward affordable at multi-million-instance weekly scale: a DPO-style pairwise objective restricts the gradient pass to a small top-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>/bottom-<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> subset of sampled trajectories, and a phase structure reduces trainer<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>inference-server parameter syncs from per-step to per-phase. An automated promotion gate over reward-like and stability metrics detected and recovered from a real reward-hacking incident in production. Rewriter output is consumed as parallel relevance signals at recall, rawrank, and finerank without displacing the original signals, bounding rewriter-failure blast radius. Online A/B from two sequential production launches—first deploying the rewriter at finerank, then extending consumption to recall and rawrank—delivers statistically significant reductions in change-query rate on rewrite-impacted queries, with all headline relevance and engagement metrics moving in the expected direction.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>query rewriting, large language models, preference optimization, mixed preference optimization, semi-online reinforcement learning, industrial deployment, video search
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In short-video <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">search-after-view</em>—queries issued by users immediately after browsing a personalized feed—the user’s last-viewed feed document <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{last}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{last}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">narrow context</em> for interpreting the query, and the user’s posterior behavior in the same session (strict clicks and skips on displayed documents) provides <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dense posterior feedback</em> about which interpretations were actually useful. This combination—narrow context plus dense posterior feedback—combines in a way unusually informative for training a query rewriter. Earlier in-house work in this setting established that even crude crowd-aggregated context signals (the SAR baseline in our offline comparisons, Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Long-Term Continuous Training ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) materially improve ranking quality; this paper takes the same insight much further by training a 7B-parameter LLM-based rewriter that emits a context-aware reformulation <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}</annotation></semantics></math> per <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,d_{\text{last}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,d_{\text{last}})</annotation></semantics></math> tuple, against a reward function constructed from precisely these two signals. The resulting system, which we refer to as <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoRe</span> (Context Relevance), has been continuously updated and redeployed every week for over five months in the search system of a major short-video platform, with rewrite-derived signals consumed in parallel at three ranking stages (recall, rawrank, finerank). Its design rests on four mutually reinforcing contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(1) A posterior-aligned reward construction</em> (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). A query rewriter has no gold-standard label, so the training signal must be induced from <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in-session posterior behavior</em>. We use the deployed multimodal relevance model to score the rewrite against this posterior on three document pools (positives, hard negatives, and random easy negatives), combined via a multiplicative reward whose form mirrors the production ranking-fusion factor; the hard- and easy-negatives ratios align with the rerank and recall stages respectively. The algebraic match means the rewriter is trained to optimize a monotone, multiplicatively-structured function of the same per-document score <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tilde{q},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tilde{q},d)</annotation></semantics></math> that the serving fusion consumes, closing the simulation–production gap that offline reward proxies (offline rerankers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, retrieval metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) leave open.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(2) A semi-online MPO training loop</em> (Sections <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Semi-Online Mixed Preference Optimization ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>–<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. MPO Loss ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>). Learning evolving user interests is fundamentally data-scale: capturing the breadth of posterior behavior demands a large preference set per training cycle for adequate generalization. Fully on-policy methods like GRPO are impractical at this scale—they take a gradient over every rolled-out trajectory and sync inference-server parameters every gradient step. A DPO-style pairwise objective restricts the gradient pass to a top-<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>/bottom-<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> subset, cutting trajectory count; the phase-bounded rollout schedule additionally amortizes parameter syncs and lets reward calls be scheduled off-peak. The mixed MPO loss (DPO+BCO+SFT) preserves stability over the multi-month horizon.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(3) A two-family automated promotion gate</em> (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Auto-Promoted Continuous Updating ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>) over three reward-like and four stability metrics. The stability family is structurally essential: in pilot deployment it caught a verbosity-driven reward-hacking pattern that reward-like metrics alone would have classified as continued improvement. Over the five-month window documented here, the gate has executed 20 weekly retrain cycles and promoted 16 candidates (<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math> acceptance rate)—to our knowledge the first published week-by-week account of an LLM query rewriter under an automated promotion gate over a multi-month production window.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(4) An additive parallel-path consumption pattern at all three ranking stages</em> (Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6. Production Consumption ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>). The rewriter is served near-line into a KV cache and consumed as a parallel signal at recall, rawrank, and finerank, never as a replacement: cache-miss and rewriter outage degrade ranking back to baseline rather than breaking it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The rewriter has been deployed in two sequential production launches: a <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finerank launch</em> that activates the rewriter as a context-relevance node inside the finerank stage, and a subsequent <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">recall+rawrank launch</em> that additionally activates a context-relevance retriever at recall and a fusion factor at rawrank. Both launches deliver statistically significant reductions in change-query rate on rewrite-impacted queries and improvements across headline relevance and engagement metrics. The pattern is discussed in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Online A/B by Ranking Stage ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-based query rewriting.</span> LLM rewriting was popularized by Query2Doc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and the broader pseudo-document expansion line; subsequent work explored RL-based rewriting against downstream retrieval signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Among industrial deployments, BEQUE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> on Taobao used three-stage SFT-plus-contrastive-alignment training for long-tail rewriting; MAAQR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alipay Search Team, 2025</a>)</cite> on Alipay organized multiple LLM agents around an instruction-tuned rewriter; CardRewriter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> added knowledge cards as auxiliary context on a short-video platform. Closest to ours, WeWrite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> applies GRPO to demand-aware video-search rewriting with a recall-stage “Fake Recall” deployment. We differ in three ways: (i) semi-online MPO rather than per-step GRPO, motivated by per-cycle compute and large-preference-set generalization under our weekly retraining cadence; (ii) a reward whose multiplicative form mirrors the production ranking-fusion algebra rather than a generic scalar, closing the simulation–production gap; and (iii) parallel-path deployment extended across all three of recall, rawrank, and finerank rather than the recall stage alone. MiniELM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> took the orthogonal direction of distilling the rewriter into a lightweight online model; we instead serve the full 7B model near-line with caching (Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6. Production Consumption ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward design with production-fidelity sources.</span> RaFe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> used an off-the-shelf reranker as a preference-label source; LLM-QE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> formalized this with an MRR-based reward plus an answer-based auxiliary term. Both use offline rerankers; we instead use the production relevance model itself, closing the offline–online gap. Using a deployed downstream-metric model as RL reward has been validated independently in ad-text generation at Meta <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; our setting is distinguished by the relevance model’s multimodal text-and-MM nature and by reward-source-equals-production-ranker identity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preference optimization and semi-online RL.</span> DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> introduced the offline preference-tuning paradigm; MPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gou and Nguyen, 2024</a>)</cite> mixed preference and supervised signals; the InternVL2-MPO formulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> we adopt combines DPO, BCO, and SFT in one objective. Iterative variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> showed that re-collecting preferences against the current policy recovers much of fully online RL, and recent analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanchantin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> characterizes the semi-online regime between fully offline and fully online preference tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Industrial precedents.</span> LLM-based components in search and recommendation have been documented in Pinterest’s LLM-relevance distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, LinkedIn’s LiRank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borisyuk et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Douyin’s multimodal ranking and content-understanding stack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We extend this line with a week-by-week account of an LLM rewriter under a multi-month auto-promotion window (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Long-Term Continuous Training ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>System Overview</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. System Overview ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives an end-to-end view of <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoRe</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the rest of the paper, we use the following notation. A user’s raw query is <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the last document the user viewed in their personalized feed before issuing the query is <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{last}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{last}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the rewriter is a generative model <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})</annotation></semantics></math> instantiated as a 7B-parameter Mistral backbone. For a candidate rewrite <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and a candidate document <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, the deployed online text-and-multimodal relevance model returns a relevance score <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tilde{q},d)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tilde{q},d)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> formed as a fixed weighted sum of a text subscore and a multimodal subscore. Per training instance, <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> denote positive and negative document pools selected from session logs by thresholding aggregated per-document engagement statistics (construction in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), and <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>rand</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a small additional pool sampled uniformly from the production corpus, disjoint from <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{+}\cup\mathcal{D}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{+}\cup\mathcal{D}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, used by the reward function as an easy-negatives reference. Per training instance the rewriter samples <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate rewrites from <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, each capped at the serving-time length budget <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>. All specific hyperparameter values referenced symbolically below are collected in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Hyperparameters and Implementation Details ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The cached rewrite feeds three additive parallel-path consumers at the online ranking stages: a context-relevance retriever at recall, an <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{L}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{L}0</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{L}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{L}1</annotation></semantics></math> fusion factor at rawrank, and a context-relevance node at finerank—each augmenting rather than replacing the original relevance signal. Cache misses and rewriter outages fall back to the original ranking pathway without interrupting service.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> details the four method components in order: the production-aligned reward construction (§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), the semi-online MPO training loop and loss (§<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Semi-Online Mixed Preference Optimization ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>–§<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. MPO Loss ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>), the auto-promotion gate and continuous-update protocol (§<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Auto-Promoted Continuous Updating ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>), and the additive parallel-path deployment pattern (§<a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6. Production Consumption ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14127v1/fig1_system_v4.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/316;" width="405" height="316"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>End-to-end system. Five blocks: (1) weekly offline training loop producing candidate <math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; (2) production-aligned reward computation ingesting content-safety scores <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{S}_{1\text{--}4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{S}_{1\text{--}4}</annotation></semantics></math>, behavioral weights <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>±</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the deployed multimodal text-and-MM relevance score <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>; (3) auto-promotion gate over three reward-like and four stability metrics; (4) near-line rewrite inference feeding an in-memory KV cache; and (5) additive parallel-path consumption at recall, rawrank, and finerank, where the rewriter contributes a new signal alongside the original signals rather than replacing them. Cache-miss falls back to the original ranking pathway, bounding the blast radius of rewriter failures.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the task of generating a context-aware rewrite of a user’s raw query in a short-video search engine. Concretely, when a user issues a search query <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> shortly after viewing a document <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{last}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{last}}</annotation></semantics></math> in their personalized feed, we wish to emit a rewritten query <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the deployed online relevance model assigns higher scores to documents the user will subsequently engage with than to documents they will skip. The rewriter is a generative model <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})</annotation></semantics></math> instantiated as a 7B-parameter open language model. The defining difficulty of this task is the absence of any gold-standard rewrite: no annotator can reliably judge whether one rewrite is “better” than another in a user-intent sense, and the only signal that ground-truths the rewriter’s value is how the downstream ranking system behaves when it is fed <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. We therefore formulate rewriter training as preference optimization over sampled candidate rewrites, with the preferences induced by the joint behavior of the production relevance model and observed user actions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Production-Aligned Reward Construction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our reward function is the principal place in which we encode three production-alignment commitments: that the reward source is the same multimodal relevance model that ranks documents online, that the reward’s algebraic form mirrors the production ranking-fusion algebra, and that the reward incorporates posterior behavioral signals weighted to reflect their economic value to the search system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pair construction.</span> A training instance is a prompt context <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,d_{\text{last}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,d_{\text{last}})</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from a weekly window of session logs; the document pools <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{+},\mathcal{D}^{-},\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>rand</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{+},\mathcal{D}^{-},\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the instance enter only at reward scoring (defined next). From the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampled rewrites per instance we form preference pairs via two rejection-sampling rules. The default branch takes the top-<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> highest- and bottom-<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> lowest-reward rewrites and forms <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times k</annotation></semantics></math> pairs from their cross product (with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> in our deployed configuration, yielding 4 candidate pairs per instance), but a pair <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{q}^{+},\tilde{q}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{q}^{+},\tilde{q}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math> is retained only if the reward gap <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\tilde{q}^{+})-r(\tilde{q}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\tilde{q}^{+})-r(\tilde{q}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds a pair-level margin <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{pair}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>pair</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{pair}}</annotation></semantics></math>; pairs with smaller gaps are rejected as uninformative. A separate raw-query branch handles instances on which the raw query itself is already near-optimal: if <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(q)&gt;\max_{m}r(\tilde{q}^{(m)})+\delta_{\mathrm{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>raw</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(q)&gt;\max_{m}r(\tilde{q}^{(m)})+\delta_{\mathrm{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a fixed margin <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>raw</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace the default branch with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> preference pairs in which the raw query <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the single chosen instance, paired individually against <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> rewrites sampled uniformly without replacement from the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-rollout pool as the rejected instances. This branch prevents training on instances where deviation from the raw query is itself the wrong move. For the initial training, a four-week aggregation window in our production region yields approximately <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.4\cdot 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4\cdot 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> training instances, an order of magnitude larger than what was available during an earlier pilot in a smaller region; weekly continuous updates use a single-week slice (Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Auto-Promoted Continuous Updating ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward function.</span> The reward is computed against three document pools associated with each training instance. The first two—the <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strict-click positive pool</em> <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no-action negative pool</em> <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>—are constructed at the document level from the same weekly session-log window: for each candidate document we aggregate posterior engagement statistics across its impressions and apply thresholds. <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> collects documents whose aggregated strict-click rate exceeds a positive threshold; <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> collects those whose aggregated no-action rate exceeds a negative threshold. The third pool, the <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">random-document pool</em> <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>rand</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consists of five documents sampled uniformly from the production document corpus, disjoint from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{+}\cup\mathcal{D}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{+}\cup\mathcal{D}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first two jointly provide <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in-session contrastive signals</em>: <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is the click-engaged positive set, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is the no-action <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hard-negative</em> set (documents the user was shown but explicitly chose not to engage with), and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>rand</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">easy negatives</em>—arbitrary corpus documents almost surely irrelevant to the query. Maintaining both negative pools is a deliberate alignment with the rewriter’s three-stage deployment (Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6. Production Consumption ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>): hard negatives are the contrast set faced by the rerank stages, while easy negatives are the contrast set faced by recall; training against both keeps the policy from over-specializing to the rerank regime that in-session negatives alone would otherwise privilege. We adopt this hard/easy <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> rerank/recall mapping as a design rationale grounded in the standard recall-vs-rerank distinction in IR rather than as an empirically isolated finding; an online ablation isolating the easy-negatives ratio’s recall-stage contribution would be the natural way to falsify it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For a candidate rewrite <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tilde{q},d)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tilde{q},d)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the relevance score returned by the deployed online text-and-multimodal relevance model. For each positive document <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{D}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{D}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{+}_{i}=\mathrm{strictCTR}(d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>strictCTR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{+}_{i}=\mathrm{strictCTR}(d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and for each hard negative <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathcal{D}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathcal{D}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{-}_{j}=\mathrm{noActionRate}(d_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>noActionRate</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{-}_{j}=\mathrm{noActionRate}(d_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> (the same per-document rates used as inclusion thresholds above). Random documents carry no posterior weight since they were never shown to the user. Define the behavior-weighted averages on the strict-click and no-action pools, and the unweighted average on the random pool, as</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;\frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}^{+}}w^{+}_{i}\,s(\tilde{q},d_{i})}{\sum_{i}w^{+}_{i}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;\frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}^{+}}w^{+}_{i}\,s(\tilde{q},d_{i})}{\sum_{i}w^{+}_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{\mathrm{neg}}(\tilde{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{\mathrm{neg}}(\tilde{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;\frac{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}^{-}}w^{-}_{j}\,s(\tilde{q},d_{j})}{\sum_{j}w^{-}_{j}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;\frac{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}^{-}}w^{-}_{j}\,s(\tilde{q},d_{j})}{\sum_{j}w^{-}_{j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E1Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{\mathrm{rand}}(\tilde{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{\mathrm{rand}}(\tilde{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}|}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}}s(\tilde{q},d_{k})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>rand</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>rand</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}|}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{rand}}}s(\tilde{q},d_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Our base reward combines the hard-negatives ratio with an easy-negatives ratio:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{base}}(\tilde{q})\;=\;\mathrm{clip}\!\left[\,\frac{\bigl(S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})\bigr)^{1+\gamma}}{S_{\mathrm{neg}}(\tilde{q})}\,\cdot\,\biggl(\frac{S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})}{S_{\mathrm{rand}}(\tilde{q})}\biggr)^{\!\alpha_{R}}\,\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>base</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.392em" rspace="0.392em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{base}}(\tilde{q})\;=\;\mathrm{clip}\!\left[\,\frac{\bigl(S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})\bigr)^{1+\gamma}}{S_{\mathrm{neg}}(\tilde{q})}\,\cdot\,\biggl(\frac{S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})}{S_{\mathrm{rand}}(\tilde{q})}\biggr)^{\!\alpha_{R}}\,\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and clip bounds set as in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Hyperparameters and Implementation Details ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. Three design choices in Equation <a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> deserve elaboration. First, the multiplicative ratio form is not arbitrary; it satisfies two requirements simultaneously. <em id="S4.SS2.p4.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Goal alignment</em>: the rewriter’s only lever for influencing ranking is the relevance model and the fusion factor that consumes it; constructing the reward to mirror this same fusion algebra is equivalent to training the rewriter directly against the engagement-ranked relevance objective. <em id="S4.SS2.p4.4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Algorithm–system consistency</em>: the quantity the rewriter is trained to optimize is a monotone, multiplicatively-structured function of the same per-document score <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tilde{q},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tilde{q},d)</annotation></semantics></math> that determines its downstream ranking impact at serving time. The resulting reward avoids a class of train–serve mismatches that no amount of additional rewriter capacity can correct—a substantively underused design lever in the LLM-rewrite literature. Second, the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1{+}\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1{+}\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> exponent on the weighted-positive term places mild emphasis on increasing relevance to high-value documents rather than merely depressing relevance to negatives. Third, the small easy-negatives exponent <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is calibrated so the random-pool ratio supplies a secondary recall-aligned signal without dominating the gradient: easy negatives are by construction quickly outranked once the policy is even marginally relevant, and a larger <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> would let this trivially-attainable signal swamp the harder rerank-aligned signal in the first ratio. Closely related reward-shape ideas have appeared in the general RLHF literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
but to our knowledge no prior query-rewriting system has documented this specific construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Production-fidelity reward source and safety filtering.</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tilde{q},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tilde{q},d)</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is the deployed multimodal relevance score (Sections <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S2" title="2. Related Work ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>; Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Hyperparameters and Implementation Details ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). To prevent the reward from privileging rewrites whose top documents are content-safety violations, we additionally remove from each positive pool the top 2% of candidates by an aggregate of four internal content-safety scores (<math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{S}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{S}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{S}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{S}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, covering sexual and sensitive content).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A final reward-shape modification, a length-and-stop penalty</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\tilde{q})\;=\;r_{\mathrm{base}}(\tilde{q})\cdot\min\!\bigl(L_{\text{target}}/L,\,1\bigr)^{\lambda_{L}}\cdot\beta_{\text{cut}}^{\,\mathbb{1}_{\text{cut}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>base</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mtext>cut</mtext><msub><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck"> 1</mn><mtext>cut</mtext></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\tilde{q})\;=\;r_{\mathrm{base}}(\tilde{q})\cdot\min\!\bigl(L_{\text{target}}/L,\,1\bigr)^{\lambda_{L}}\cdot\beta_{\text{cut}}^{\,\mathbb{1}_{\text{cut}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the rewrite token length, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is a small length-penalty exponent (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Hyperparameters and Implementation Details ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>), and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{1}_{\text{cut}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mtext>cut</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{1}_{\text{cut}}</annotation></semantics></math> is 1 iff generation was truncated by the length cap (as opposed to terminating on the EOS token), was added several months into continuous training in response to a documented case of reward hacking via verbosity. The small exponents on both the length-penalty factor and the cut-penalty base are deliberate: each individual rewrite is penalized only mildly, but the penalties accumulate sharply on a policy whose entire output distribution drifts toward verbose / cap-truncated generations. We defer that story to Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Auto-Promoted Continuous Updating ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, where it serves as concrete motivation for the auto-promotion gate’s stability metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Semi-Online Mixed Preference Optimization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Per-cycle wall-clock is the binding constraint in our setting: learning evolving user interests demands a large preference set per training cycle, and the weekly retraining cadence caps how long each cycle can take. Two architectural properties of fully on-policy methods like GRPO make them impractical in this regime, and a third consideration constrains the choice of loss within the off-policy family.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">First, gradient-pass cost (primary).</em> Because each rewrite is short (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>), per-step wall-clock is dominated by the gradient pass rather than rollout generation—the opposite of typical long-CoT RLHF. GRPO computes a policy gradient over all <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> rolled-out trajectories per instance; a DPO-style pairwise objective passes only the top-<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>/bottom-<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> subset. In the gradient-dominated regime, this trajectory-count reduction is the principal source of training efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Second, the phase structure addresses two supporting bottlenecks.</em> (i) Fully on-policy methods sync trainer-to-inference-server parameters at every gradient step; per-event the cost is small (seconds on a dense 7B with an optimized transport), but cumulative over tens of thousands of weekly gradient steps it is non-trivial—the phase schedule replaces <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\text{steps})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>steps</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\text{steps})</annotation></semantics></math> sync events with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\text{phases})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>phases</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\text{phases})</annotation></semantics></math>. (ii) The same phase boundary decouples reward-call timing from per-gradient-step timing: the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!\times\!\lvert\mathcal{T}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!\times\!\lvert\mathcal{T}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> reward calls per phase can be batched into off-peak windows where they do not contend with the production relevance model’s online serving capacity. The total number of reward calls is unchanged, but their scheduling becomes flexible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Third, long-term iteration stability (loss-form choice).</em> The multi-month continuous-training horizon (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Long-Term Continuous Training ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) made empirical stability of the loss form material; we found the mixed MPO loss (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. MPO Loss ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>) more stable than per-step preference-gradient methods, a property we attribute to the loss form rather than to phase structure per se.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a semi-online formulation in which each phase’s preference set is constructed once and reused across an MPO epoch (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.3. Semi-Online Mixed Preference Optimization ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Training proceeds in phases <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1,\dots,P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1,\dots,P</annotation></semantics></math>: at the start of each phase we sample <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidates per instance from <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{p-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{p-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, score them under Equation <a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, build top-<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>/bottom-<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> preference pairs, and run one MPO epoch. Cosine warm restarts at phase boundaries empirically stabilize the optimizer across partially overlapping preference distributions. Recent analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanchantin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> characterizes when semi-online preference optimization closes most of the gap to fully on-policy training; our experience is consistent.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14127v1/fig2_semionline_v2.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/290;" width="429" height="290"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Semi-online MPO phase loop. Within a phase <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts per training instance are sampled once and scored, and only a small preference-pair subset (top-<math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> chosen <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> bottom-<math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> rejected) is reused across many gradient steps in one MPO epoch; phase boundaries apply a cosine warm restart on the learning rate. Restricting the gradient pass to the preference-pair subset—rather than to all rolled-out trajectories—is the principal source of training-step efficiency relative to per-step preference-gradient methods.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix D Algorithm: Semi-Online MPO Reward Finetuning ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Algorithm: Semi-Online MPO Reward Finetuning ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> summarizes the procedure. The phase structure also permits in-place migration of the reward source: during the iteration documented here we swapped a text-only relevance model for the deployed text-and-multimodal model after several phases, only regenerating <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> under the new reward.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>MPO Loss</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MPO loss we adopt is the mixed formulation introduced by Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>
in the multimodal reasoning context, which combines a Direct Preference Optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>
term, a Binary Classification Optimization (BCO) term, and a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) regularizer in a single objective. The DPO term provides the gradient signal that ranks <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> above <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>; the BCO term provides absolute calibration on the chosen rewrite, mitigating the well-known DPO failure mode in which both preferred and dispreferred responses are jointly suppressed; and the SFT term anchors the policy near rewrites that previously achieved high reward. Concretely, for a preference pair <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{q}^{+},\tilde{q}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{q}^{+},\tilde{q}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math> in context <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,d_{\text{last}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,d_{\text{last}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss is</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MPO}}\;=\;w_{p}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}\;+\;w_{q}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BCO}}\;+\;w_{g}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SFT}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>MPO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>DPO</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>BCO</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MPO}}\;=\;w_{p}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}\;+\;w_{q}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BCO}}\;+\;w_{g}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SFT}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with components, in terms of the log-ratio <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}):=\log\!\bigl(\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})\,/\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}):=\log\!\bigl(\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})\,/\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\tilde{q}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>DPO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{DPO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,[r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{+})-r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{-})]\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,[r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{+})-r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{-})]\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BCO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>BCO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{BCO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{+})-\delta_{\mathrm{BCO}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>BCO</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{+})-\delta_{\mathrm{BCO}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phantom{={}}-\log\!\bigl(1-\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{-})-\delta_{\mathrm{BCO}}\bigr)\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>BCO</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phantom{={}}-\log\!\bigl(1-\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,r_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{-})-\delta_{\mathrm{BCO}}\bigr)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\log\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{+}\mid q,d_{\text{last}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\log\pi_{\theta}(\tilde{q}^{+}\mid q,d_{\text{last}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Component weights <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{p},w_{q},w_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{p},w_{q},w_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, the DPO temperature <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and the BCO threshold <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{BCO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>BCO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{BCO}}</annotation></semantics></math> are set as in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Hyperparameters and Implementation Details ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> (the BCO threshold follows Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>). The mixed objective is essential at our scale: a controlled comparison during initial training showed pure DPO drifting into the joint-suppression failure mode (chosen-trajectory log-probability collapsing alongside rejected), whereas MPO maintained absolute calibration via BCO and distributional anchoring via SFT (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B DPO Joint-Suppression Drift at Scale ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Earlier MPO work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gou and Nguyen, 2024</a>)</cite> established the case for mixing preference and supervised signals; the InternVL2-MPO formulation we adopt is, to our knowledge, the first application of this specific mixture to query rewriting. Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. Ablations ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> reports the corresponding offline comparison against pure DPO, SFT, SFT-then-DPO, and SimPO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate the rewriter on a 7B-parameter open Mistral backbone; phase scheduling is orchestrated on our internal ML training platform.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Auto-Promoted Continuous Updating</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At convergence of the procedure described above, the rewriter is suitable for an initial production deployment. The contribution we describe in this subsection concerns what happens after that deployment: a weekly retraining cadence, gated by a multi-metric promotion rule, that has been running in production for over five months at the time of writing. The weekly cadence reflects the rewriter’s character as a semantic generator—closer to a language model than to a CTR-based discriminative personalizer—whose drift rate is slow enough that weekly batch retraining suffices, without paying the engineering cost of streaming or per-day updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pipeline.</span> Unlike the initial <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-phase training of Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Semi-Online Mixed Preference Optimization ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, each weekly continuous-update run is a single-phase, single-epoch retrain from the previous week’s deployed checkpoint <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. We pull a fresh weekly slice of session logs, reconstruct preference pairs under the same reward as in Equation <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and run a single MPO epoch to produce a candidate <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are then evaluated on a held-out slice of the following week’s logs (i.e., never on the data used to train <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>), and the promotion rule described next is applied. If <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is promoted it becomes the next week’s <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is held and the failed candidate is logged for diagnosis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two metric families.</span> The promotion rule operates over two complementary families of metrics. The first, which we call <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward-like</em>, are intermediate quantities that the reward function (Equations <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) already produces during training; we recompute them on the held-out evaluation slice without modification, and track them individually as separate auto-eval inputs. Concretely: <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{main}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>main</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{main}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the full scalar <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\tilde{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\tilde{q})</annotation></semantics></math> of Equation <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>; <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the positive-pool average <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})</annotation></semantics></math> of Equation <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; and <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{rand}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{rand}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the easy-negatives ratio <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})/S_{\mathrm{rand}}(\tilde{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{pos}}(\tilde{q})/S_{\mathrm{rand}}(\tilde{q})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the base of the second multiplicative factor in Equation <a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reported here without the <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> exponent. None of these is an eval-specific quantity; rather, the auto-eval gate exposes them <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">separately</em> so that a candidate which has improved the dominant scalar reward but silently regressed on the positive-pool absolute relevance (<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>) or on the recall-aligned easy-negatives signal (<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{rand}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>rand</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{rand}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is held rather than promoted. The second family, <em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stability</em>, captures whether the candidate rewriter has begun to exhibit pathological generation behavior independently of the reward signal: <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">top10_entropy</span> measures the Shannon entropy of the renormalized top-10 token distribution at each generation step, serving as a sharpness-drift detector; <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">surprisal</span> is the mean per-token negative log-probability of the generated rewrites under the candidate, serving as a catastrophic-divergence guard; <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">avg_token_length</span> is the average rewrite length, a verbosity guard; and <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">stop_rate</span> is the fraction of rewrites terminated by the length cap, a reward-hacking guard. Together these seven metrics constitute the entirety of our promotion-decision input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Promotion rule.</span> A candidate <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is promoted if and only if all three reward-like metrics strictly improve over <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> on the held-out slice and all four stability metrics fall within configured operating bounds. The conjunction is intentionally strict: a candidate that improves the headline reward at the cost of, say, a doubled stop-rate is held, on the principle that long-run drift is rarely worth a one-week reward bump. Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the cumulative gate-acceptance rate over the five-month period.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward hacking via verbosity, and the length-penalty fix.</span> The motivating story for the second metric family comes from pilot deployment, before the operating window in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Long-Term Continuous Training ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> opens. The stability monitor flagged a slow week-over-week increase in <span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">avg_token_length</span> alongside a sharp rise in <span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">stop_rate</span> toward the length cap, while reward-like metrics were still improving. Inspection revealed two compounding mechanisms by which the policy was exploiting the reward function—one inside the cap (hedging-driven <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> inflation) and one at the cap (truncation invisibility to the reward); Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Verbosity Reward-Hacking: Full Mechanism Analysis ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> gives the full analysis. The two factors in Equation <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> each address one mechanism. The resulting penalty has been part of the deployed reward throughout the operating window in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Long-Term Continuous Training ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>; Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2. Long-Term Continuous Training ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports a controlled before/after demonstration. We highlight this episode not because the specific penalty form is novel but for its generalizable lesson: stability metrics are a structural necessity, because the gradient of the reward function is by construction blind to the ways in which the reward function itself is being gamed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6. </span>Production Consumption</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A trained rewriter is only useful insofar as the production ranking pipeline can consume it without operational risk. Two design choices govern that consumption: a near-line serving architecture that decouples rewrite generation from the online critical path, and an additive consumption pattern that injects rewrite-derived relevance signals into all three ranking stages without ever displacing the original signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Near-line architecture.</span> The 7B rewriter is served on dedicated inference instances. Rather than blocking on rewrite generation at request time, our pipeline triggers asynchronously on each <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,d_{\text{last}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,d_{\text{last}})</annotation></semantics></math> event: the rewriter produces <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}</annotation></semantics></math> shortly after the event lands and the result is cached in an in-memory key–value store keyed by <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,d_{\text{last,id}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last,id</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,d_{\text{last,id}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Online ranking, when it next handles a matching request, performs a cache lookup; if the rewrite is available it is consumed as described below, and on cache miss the ranking pipeline proceeds with the original relevance signals only. In steady state on production traffic, the cache lookup achieves over <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> coverage on rewriter-impacted requests, with the residual cache misses corresponding largely to first-occurrence <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,d_{\text{last}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>last</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,d_{\text{last}})</annotation></semantics></math> events. This non-blocking architecture means that rewrite latency, rewrite generation failures, and even total rewriter outage degrade ranking quality back towards baseline rather than breaking the pipeline. We run two parallel near-line pipelines in front of two serving instances, which simplifies A/B comparison and rolling deployment of new rewriter checkpoints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additive parallel-path consumption.</span> The rewriter’s output is consumed at all three ranking stages—recall, rawrank, and finerank—and at every stage the rewrite contributes a <em id="S4.SS6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">new parallel signal</em> layered on top of the existing relevance machinery, never a replacement for it. At recall, a new context-relevance retriever takes <math id="S4.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}</annotation></semantics></math> as its query and contributes its candidates to the union of the original retrievers. At rawrank, a new <math id="S4.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{L}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{L}0</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{L}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{L}1</annotation></semantics></math> fusion factor formed from <math id="S4.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tilde{q},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tilde{q},d)</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied into the existing rawrank scoring formula. At finerank, a context-relevance node feeds <math id="S4.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\tilde{q},d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\tilde{q},d)</annotation></semantics></math> into the same multiplicative full-relevance factor described in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>—closing the algebraic loop with our reward design. The closest published architecture to ours is the parallel “Fake Recall” pattern of WeWrite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
which applies a similar isolation strategy at the recall stage; our contribution is to extend the same bounded-blast-radius pattern across the entire ranking pipeline. The combined effect of the near-line cache fallback and the additive parallel-path layout is that adding the rewriter to production is, in the limit, never worse than not adding it: the original relevance machinery remains intact, and the rewriter contributes only when its output is available and the additional signal proves informative.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate the rewriter on a 7B-parameter open Mistral backbone and train it on session logs from a single production region of a major short-video search engine. The reward function (Equation <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is computed using the multimodal relevance model deployed at the time of the corresponding training phase. Online evaluation is conducted by bucketed A/B testing on production traffic, with each launch group consuming between 10% and 30% of randomly assigned users. Headline online metrics across all launches include strict click-through rate (Strict CTR), long-click CTR, change-query ratio, and avg relevance factor. Unless otherwise noted, all online metrics reported in this section are measured on the slice of queries on which the rewriter actually emitted a rewrite (the rewrite-impacted-query slice).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Long-Term Continuous Training</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-continuous phases.</span> Before the auto-promotion loop took over, the rewriter went through four offline semi-online MPO phases in the production region (Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2. Long-Term Continuous Training ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The reward function (Eq. <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, all hyperparameters as in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Production-Aligned Reward Construction ‣ 4. Method ‣ CoRe: A Continuously Reward-Finetuned LLM Query Rewriter for Multi-Stage Context-Aware Relevance in Web-Scale Video Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) is held fixed across phases, so values are directly comparable. We report two reward summaries (an unweighted average; a page-view-weighted average that tracks the production-traffic distribution) and a “Rewrite Improvement Rate” (RIR), the fraction of evaluation instances on which the rewrite’s reward exceeds that of the raw query. Each of the four phases contributes a roughly equal increment, consistent with the semi-online interpretation that fresh phase rollouts surface new high-reward patterns the prior phase did not cover.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Offline reward progression across the four pre-continuous semi-online MPO phases. “SAR baseline” is a previous-generation context-aware query-derivation method without LLM rewriting. Phase <math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the <math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-th semi-online MPO phase in our production region. PV-w = page-view-weighted; RIR = rewrite improvement rate.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Model</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Reward (avg)</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Reward (PV-w)</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">RIR (%)</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SAR baseline</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1.055</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1.032</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">47.4</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
